+# FAA's catalog dates the Audit Followup order's cancellation to September 30, 2006; its text remains unavailable
+
+October 29, 2026, America/New_York. B129 / PD04. **A newly inspected official FAA catalog record displays a September 30, 2006 cancellation date for Order 2900.1, Audit Followup.** Its issue date, title and ABU-100 office value correspond to the earlier checklist row. This is a specific current agency-catalog observation and historical-version retrieval lead, not an acquired cancellation instrument, successor policy or verified governing rule for the 9/11 investigation.
+
+## Exact current source and values
+
+The [official record, document ID 7592](https://www.faa.gov/regulations_policies/orders_notices/index.cfm/go/document.information/documentID/7592), displays the following fields in its **Document Information** definition list:
+
+| Field label | Displayed value |
+| --- | --- |
+| Type | Order |
+| Number | 2900.1 |
+| Title | Audit Followup |
+| Date issued | **1985-07-05** |
+| Date cancelled | **2006-09-30** |
+| Cancellation notes | **AFM-100** |
+| Office of Primary Responsibility | **ABU-100** |
+| Access restriction | **Public** |
+
+The **Content** section says the document's content is unavailable. Both the Public access label and the unavailable-content message remain preserved. Public is a catalog access category, not proof that a downloadable body is presently offered; the unavailable message is not a withholding determination, destruction record or evidence the original never existed. The contact route was not used.
+
+The raw HTML entity was acquired on **October 30 UTC / October 29 America/New_York**, with HTTP 200 and **107,152 bytes**, SHA-256 **06e0b724fbf10b170de0720c52e21ca7b5cd956e4528d1a635e55e85d2e5a32b**. The definition-list labels and their adjacent values were checked in the visible page and the preserved HTML. A current webpage/hash does not independently authenticate the historical effective date, cancellation approval, amendments or retention history.
+
+## Why the identifier and filter matter
+
+The default Active-status lookup for 2900.1 returned no result. The subsequent **All-status** lookup returned two entries: **Audit Followup**, ABU-100, dated July 5, 1985; and **Audits by the Gao & the Oig**, ANM-40, dated October 1, 1983. Both rows were displayed as cancelled. These are distinct catalog entries with the same number; this review does not merge their scope, dates or text, or infer that one supersedes the other.
+
+The first attempt to choose All using keyboard navigation did not change the selector; that attempt remained Active. The successful lookup explicitly selected and verified All before submission. This is a research-method control, not an agency inconsistency. A default filtered miss cannot establish historical absence or suppression. The actual [all-status query](https://www.faa.gov/regulations_policies/orders_notices/index.cfm/go/document.list/?q=2900.1&documentTypeID=&statusID=) is a current search result, not a complete historical inventory.
+
+The match to the earlier **Audit Followup / 1985-07-05 / ABU-100** checklist row makes document 7592 a specific candidate for the missing policy/version file. It does not authenticate the text received or applied by a 2006 reviewer. The separate ANM-40 entry is not used to fill the missing text. The current metadata also does not by itself establish historical nationwide scope; title/office/date correspondence is kept separate from native version and applicability.
+
+## The policy-period question is now more precise
+
+The [August 31, 2006 investigation report](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=12) recommends correction of the Commission response and consideration of administrative action. The catalog now displays the later **September 30, 2006** cancellation date for the FAA implementing-order candidate. Separately, the newly acquired [November 14, 2006 financial-audit memorandum](https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/about/plans_reports/archive/Combined_FY_2006_FAA_PAR_Final_v6_12-14-2006.pdf#page=106) cites the parent **DOT Order 8000.1C** for audit follow-up.
+
+These are different records, dates and policy levels. Their ordering does not establish that the investigation had no governing follow-up duty, that the parent DOT order was cancelled, that any official evaded a duty, or that the cancellation related to the investigation. The concrete question is **which implementing provisions, transition instructions and routing applied to that investigation's recommendations and later disposition?** The actual text, cancellation authority, successor instructions, office-transfer and case records are still the discriminators.
+
+The current catalog could reflect an effective historical cancellation, a later entry or another administrative convention; the cancellation file is needed to test what it means. These are untested possibilities, not a selected explanation. A modern metadata field is weaker historical evidence than a contemporaneous cancellation/issuance record. Conversely, its specific date and AFM-100 note provide a better retrieval target than a generic request for FAA policies. No motive or legal breach is inferred.
+
+The prior **8000.1A / 8000.1C**, **1985 / 1989** and **ABU-100 / ABU-130** observations remain unchanged in the [directive crosswalk](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/f3a71187146eec653eab636f1e24cd1df25f4c4e/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-directive-crosswalk-2026-10-29/README.md). The **AFM-100** cancellation note is an additional source field, not a replacement OPR or an authenticated current custodian. The [same-year financial-audit citation review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/db86a3fb3530d4c8e207f6f3a9c11bd5f3a8b1cf/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-2006-policy-citation-2026-10-29/README.md) also remains unchanged; it does not supply the investigation's governing text or outcome.
+
+## Exact acquisition limit and remaining TODOs
+
+One exact public Wayback CDX network request for the observed 7592 page, filtering indexed status 200 and collapsing digest, returned **an empty JSON array**. That is the result of that particular URL/filter query, not an exhaustive archive search, evidence of historical deletion or proof no original policy survives. No capture was followed, no policy PDF was guessed, and no host/path variants or authentication bypass were attempted. A preceding web-reader access limitation and a local parser import failure are technical failures, not origin evidence; the latter happened before any archive network request. Local receipts preserve them and the successful standard-library recovery.
+
+**PD04 now has a specific additional TODO:** the actual Order 2900.1 text and version/amendment file linked to document 7592; the cancellation action and date support; the meaning and routing behind the AFM-100 note; any successor/transition instrument; and the actual investigation's applicable-policy/assignment/notice records. Completed FAA Form 1320-6 issuance/change and reference-set transfer records remain separate targets. The catalog alone closes none of these requirements.
+
+**PD01–PD03 remain open:** corrected QFR and recipient receipt; signed consideration/action/no-action and reasons/notification; case-specific response, resolution, implementation and closure history. The original five tracks and eighteen input requirements remain unfinished and unchanged. Missing originals defer their dependent tests, not every other authorized source inquiry. No scope exception or goal-completion claim follows from this partial source-locator advance.
+
+## Actual reading, verification and publication scope
+
+Read the current library controls, the number-query results and all relevant detail-page fields/body-availability message. This is HTML/browser inspection, not a policy-PDF or historical-cancellation reading. The earlier checklist, target investigation report and financial-audit passages are reused from their preserved reviews; they are not claimed newly reread in this unit. The official series/finance navigation lists were viewed only for source navigation and are not treated as complete inventories or historical office charts.
+
+[SOURCES.json](SOURCES.json) records URL/access/entity hash and exact definition-list locators. [OBSERVATIONS.json](OBSERVATIONS.json) pins observations to this report's lines. [OPEN-TODOS.json](OPEN-TODOS.json) preserves the four priorities and the new missing-version targets. [SOURCE-CHECKS.json](SOURCE-CHECKS.json) reproduces entity identity and selected label/value associations; it does not validate the historical cancellation date, legal applicability, reviewer knowledge or motive. [TEXT-CHANGES.diff](TEXT-CHANGES.diff) is the exact addition of these research notes; original HTML and earlier reports remain unchanged.
+
+Only reviewed original research, safe source fields, URLs, hashes and verification code/results are included. Raw HTML/browser captures, contacts, unrelated pages, signatures, private correspondence, credentials and strategy are excluded. No contact, mailbox access, fee, new request/filing, deletion, private push, merge or history rewrite occurred.
